- How would you extend to persist history across sessions without refetching pages?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of persistent data structures and O(1) updates without copying.

## Key Concepts
- Persistence via structural sharing
- Node-based linked representation
- Versioning by top pointer
- Space-time trade-offs and edge cases

## Code Example
```javascript
class Node { constructor(val, prev){ this.val = val; this.prev = prev; } }
class PersistentStack {
  constructor() { this.versions = [null]; } // version 0: empty
  push(version, val) { const top = this.versions[version]; const node = new Node(val, top); this.versions.push(node); return this.versions.length - 1; }
  pop(version) { const top = this.versions[version]; if (!top) return version; this.versions.push(top.prev); return this.versions.length - 1; }
  top(version) { const t = this.versions[version]; return t ? t.val : null; }
  isEmpty(version) { return this.versions[version] == null; }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you manage memory for old versions (garbage collection)?
- How would you expose the API to external callers (version IDs, immutability guarantees)?
- Could you adapt this to a persistent queue or other structures with similar patterns?

Why This Is Asked
- Tests dynamic graph state and a non-trivial at-query metric.

Key Concepts
- Dynamic adjacency via hash sets, on-demand intersection, edge-id tracking, amortized performance.

Code Example
```javascript
class DynamicGraph {
  constructor(){ this.adj = new Map(); this.edges = new Map(); }
  addEdge(u,v,id){ if(!this.adj.has(u)) this.adj.set(u,new Set()); if(!this.adj.has(v)) this.adj.set(v,new Set()); this.adj.get(u).add(v); this.adj.get(v).add(u); this.edges.set(id,[u,v]); }
  removeEdge(id){ const e = this.edges.get(id); if(!e) return; const [u,v] = e; this.adj.get(u).delete(v); this.adj.get(v).delete(u); this.edges.delete(id); }
  twoStepPaths(u,v){ if(u===v) return 0; const A = this.adj.get(u) || new Set(); const B = this.adj.get(v) || new Set(); if(A.size < B.size){ let c=0; for(const x of A) if(B.has(x)) c++; return c; } else { let c=0; for(const x of B) if(A.has(x)) c++; return c; } }
}
```

Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to directed graphs or multi-edges? Impact on correctness and complexity?
- What if you need to maintain this metric for many (u, v) pairs offline at once—any batching strategies?
